LESSONS = [
    ("variables", "A variable is a name that refers to a value, such as `score = 10`.",
     "In Python, `score = 10` binds the name `score` to 10. Running `score = score + 5` reads the old value, adds 5, and assigns the result, so the value becomes 15.\n\nUse descriptive names such as `total_price`. A common mistake is using a variable before assigning it: `print(total)` fails if `total` has not been defined. Also distinguish assignment, `=`, from comparison, `==`. Practice by tracing the value after each assignment, then run the code to check your prediction.",
     "Ang variable ay pangalan para sa value, tulad ng `score = 10`.",
     "Sa Python, ikinakabit ng `score = 10` ang pangalang `score` sa value na 10. Sa `score = score + 5`, kukunin muna ang dating 10, dadagdagan ng 5, at magiging 15 ang bagong value.\n\nGumamit ng malinaw na pangalan tulad ng `total_price`. Common mistake ang paggamit ng variable bago lagyan ng value. Magkaiba rin ang `=` para sa assignment at `==` para sa comparison. I-trace ang value pagkatapos ng bawat assignment bago patakbuhin ang code."),
    ("loops", "A loop repeats code for a sequence of items or while a condition holds.",
     "Use a loop when the same operation applies repeatedly:\n```python\nfor number in range(1, 4):\n    print(number)\n```\nThis prints 1, 2, and 3. The stop value 4 is excluded. A `while` loop instead repeats while its condition remains true.\n\nA common mistake is forgetting to update a counter in a `while` loop, causing it to continue indefinitely. Incorrect indentation can also place work outside the loop. Trace one iteration at a time, recording the current value and the condition that decides whether another iteration runs.",
     "Ang loop ay paulit-ulit na nagpapatakbo ng code para sa items o habang true ang condition.",
     "Halimbawa:\n```python\nfor number in range(1, 4):\n    print(number)\n```\nLalabas ang 1, 2, at 3. Hindi kasama ang 4 dahil exclusive ang stop value. Sa `while` naman, magpapatuloy habang true ang condition.\n\nCommon mistake ang hindi pag-update ng counter kaya hindi matapos ang loop. Mahalaga rin ang indentation para malaman kung anong code ang kasama sa bawat ulit. I-trace ang counter at condition pagkatapos ng bawat iteration para makita kung kailan hihinto."),
    ("functions", "A function is reusable code that can accept inputs and return a result.",
     "For example:\n```python\ndef total(price, quantity):\n    return price * quantity\n\namount = total(25, 3)\n```\n`price` and `quantity` are parameters; 25 and 3 are arguments in this call. The returned value 75 is assigned to `amount`.\n\nA common mistake is confusing `print` with `return`. Printing displays text; returning provides a value to the caller. A function that only prints a result does not automatically return that result. Test the function with ordinary inputs and boundary cases such as zero quantity.",
     "Ang function ay reusable na code na puwedeng tumanggap ng input at magbalik ng result.",
     "Halimbawa:\n```python\ndef total(price, quantity):\n    return price * quantity\n\namount = total(25, 3)\n```\nParameters ang `price` at `quantity`; arguments ang 25 at 3. Ang returned value na 75 ay mapupunta sa `amount`.\n\nCommon mistake ang pagkalito sa `print` at `return`. Nagpapakita sa screen ang `print`; ibinibigay ng `return` ang value sa caller. Hindi automatic na returned ang printed result. Subukan ang function sa normal at boundary inputs gaya ng zero quantity."),
    ("recursion", "Recursion solves a problem by calling the same function on a smaller case.",
     "A recursive function needs a base case that stops and a recursive step that makes progress. For nonnegative integers, factorial can be defined as factorial(0) = 1 and factorial(n) = n × factorial(n − 1). Thus factorial(3) is 3 × 2 × 1 × 1 = 6.\n\nA common mistake is omitting the base case or failing to reduce the input. Calls then repeat until a runtime limit is reached. Validate inputs and trace the call stack. Recursion fits tree structures well, but it is not automatically faster than a loop.",
     "Recursion ang pagtawag ng function sa sarili nito para sa mas maliit na kaso.",
     "Kailangan ng base case para huminto at recursive step na nagpapaliit sa problema. Para sa nonnegative integers, factorial(0) = 1 at factorial(n) = n × factorial(n − 1). Kaya factorial(3) = 3 × 2 × 1 × 1 = 6.\n\nCommon mistake ang walang base case o hindi nababawasan ang input. Mauulit ang calls hanggang runtime limit. I-check ang input at i-trace ang call stack. Bagay ito sa trees, pero hindi laging mas mabilis kaysa loop."),
    ("binary_search", "Binary search repeatedly halves a sorted range to locate a target.",
     "For [2, 5, 8, 12, 20] and target 12, compare with the middle value 8. Since 12 is larger, continue only in the right range. Each comparison eliminates roughly half the remaining candidates, giving logarithmic comparison growth.\n\nA common mistake is using binary search on unsorted data. Another is updating bounds without excluding the already-checked middle, which can prevent progress. Use a consistent inclusive or exclusive interval convention. Test targets that are missing, at the first position, and at the last position to catch boundary errors.",
     "Hinahati ng binary search ang sorted na range sa kalahati hanggang makita ang target.",
     "Sa [2, 5, 8, 12, 20], hanapin ang 12. Ang gitna ay 8. Dahil mas malaki ang 12, sa kanan na lang maghahanap. Halos kalahati ng candidates ang nawawala sa bawat comparison.\n\nCommon mistake ang paggamit nito sa unsorted data. Maaari ring hindi matapos kung hindi naaalis sa range ang na-check nang middle. Pumili ng consistent na interval convention at i-test ang missing target pati ang nasa first at last positions."),
    ("time_complexity", "Time complexity describes how an algorithm’s work grows with input size.",
     "Complexity concerns growth rather than fixed seconds. Visiting every item once takes work proportional to n, or O(n). Two nested loops that each run n times perform roughly n × n iterations, often O(n²).\n\nA common mistake is treating every pair of loops as quadratic. Two separate loops do n + n work, which is still O(n); nested loops can multiply the work. Check whether bounds depend on input size or are fixed. Doubling n roughly doubles linear work but can quadruple quadratic work for sufficiently large inputs.",
     "Time complexity ang paglaki ng trabaho ng algorithm habang lumalaki ang input.",
     "Growth ang pinag-uusapan, hindi eksaktong seconds. Isang pagdaan sa n items ay O(n). Kung nested ang dalawang loops at parehong n ang ulit, mga n × n iterations iyon, kaya O(n²).\n\nCommon mistake ang pagsabing quadratic basta dalawang loops. Kung magkahiwalay, n + n lang ang work, kaya O(n) pa rin. Tingnan kung fixed o dependent sa input ang bounds. Kapag dinoble ang n, halos doble ang linear work at maaaring apat na beses ang quadratic work."),
    ("sql_joins", "A SQL join combines rows from tables using a matching condition.",
     "With `students(id, name)` and `scores(student_id, score)`, an inner join on `students.id = scores.student_id` returns matching student-score pairs. A left join keeps every student, placing NULL in missing score fields.\n\nA common mistake is expecting one row per student automatically. A student with three matching score rows produces three joined rows. Use intentional aggregation when you need a summary. Draw two small tables and work out the matches by hand, including a student without a score and one with multiple scores, before applying the query to larger data.",
     "Pinagsasama ng SQL join ang rows mula sa tables gamit ang matching condition.",
     "May `students(id, name)` at `scores(student_id, score)`. Sa inner join gamit ang matching IDs, lalabas ang may katugmang score. Sa left join, kasama lahat ng students kahit walang score; NULL ang missing fields.\n\nCommon mistake ang pag-asang one row per student lagi. Kung tatlo ang score rows niya, tatlo rin ang joined rows. Kung summary ang kailangan, gumamit ng angkop na aggregation. Subukan sa maliit na tables na may missing at multiple matches."),
    ("database_transactions", "A transaction groups database operations so they can commit together or roll back on failure.",
     "When transferring 100 between two accounts, one update subtracts 100 and another adds 100. A transaction lets both changes commit together or roll back if a required operation fails, avoiding a half-completed transfer.\n\nA common mistake is assuming a successful SQL statement means the change is durable; many interfaces require an explicit commit. Transactions do not check every business rule automatically, so validate account IDs and other requirements yourself. Finally, read back the affected records to verify the outcome. Printing a success message is not evidence that the database changed.",
     "Ang transaction ay grupo ng database operations na kino-commit nang sabay o nire-roll back kapag pumalya.",
     "Sa transfer na 100, babawasan ang unang account at dadagdagan ang pangalawa. Isang transaction ang gamitin para sabay mag-commit o mag-rollback kapag may pumalya; hindi kalahati lang ang transfer.\n\nCommon mistake ang pag-asang durable agad ang successful SQL statement. Madalas kailangan pa ng commit. Hindi rin automatic ang business-rule checks tulad ng tamang account IDs. Basahin ang affected records para ma-verify ang outcome; hindi sapat ang success message."),
    ("unit_tests", "A unit test automatically checks a small behavior using known inputs and expected results.",
     "For `add(a, b)`, check that `add(2, 3)` returns 5. Arrange inputs, run the operation, and assert an observable result. For persistence code, the relevant result may be an actual saved database row rather than a return string.\n\nA common mistake is mocking away the behavior under test. Checking that a mock save function was called does not prove data was stored. Use an isolated database when testing persistence, and read the record back. Include meaningful boundary and failure cases, but avoid assertions that only mirror implementation details without checking a requirement.",
     "Ang unit test ay automatic na pag-check ng maliit na behavior gamit ang known inputs at expected result.",
     "Sa `add(a, b)`, i-check na 5 ang `add(2, 3)`. Ihanda ang input, patakbuhin ang operation, at i-check ang observable result. Sa persistence code, dapat actual saved row ang tingnan, hindi message lang.\n\nCommon mistake ang pag-mock sa mismong behavior na gustong patunayan. Hindi sapat na natawag ang mock save. Gumamit ng isolated database at i-read back ang record. Isama ang meaningful boundary at failure cases na may kaugnayan sa requirement."),
    ("train_validation_test", "Training teaches the model, validation guides development, and testing evaluates the final selected approach.",
     "Training examples update parameters. Validation examples help compare settings and select a checkpoint. Keep the test set untouched until the chosen approach is evaluated. Repeated changes based on test failures turn that set into development data.\n\nA common mistake is randomly splitting near-duplicate paraphrases, allowing variants of the same example into training and test. Group related examples into families before splitting. Record split seeds and file hashes. If failed test examples later enter training, create a new independent test set rather than continuing to describe the old score as held-out performance.",
     "Training ang pangturo, validation ang pang-guide sa development, at test ang pang-check sa final approach.",
     "Training examples ang nag-uupdate ng parameters. Validation ang pangkumpara ng settings at pagpili ng checkpoint. Itabi ang test hanggang final evaluation; kung paulit-ulit kang nagbabago base sa failures nito, nagiging development data na ito.\n\nCommon mistake ang random split ng magkakahawig na paraphrases. I-group muna sa families bago i-split. Itala ang seed at file hashes. Kung isasama sa training ang dating failed test examples, gumawa ng bagong independent test set."),
    ("overfitting", "Overfitting happens when a model fits training examples well but performs poorly on new ones.",
     "A model can memorize practice examples instead of learning patterns that generalize. Training loss may keep falling while validation performance stalls or worsens. That gap can signal overfitting.\n\nPossible responses include more diverse data, less training, regularization, and checkpoint selection based on validation. A common mistake is adding epochs simply because training accuracy is high; that can reinforce memorization. Also check for data leakage before trusting an impressive test score. If the test repeats training templates, it may hide overfitting rather than reveal whether the model handles genuinely new inputs.",
     "Overfitting kapag mahusay sa training examples pero mahina sa bagong inputs ang model.",
     "Puwedeng mamemorya ng model ang examples kaysa matutuhan ang general patterns. Bumababa pa ang training loss pero hindi na gumaganda o lumalala ang validation. Maaaring senyales iyon ng overfitting.\n\nPuwedeng dagdagan ang data diversity, bawasan ang training, gumamit ng regularization, at pumili gamit ang validation. Common mistake ang dagdag-epochs agad dahil mataas ang training accuracy. I-check din ang leakage: kung inuulit ng test ang training templates, maaaring natatago ang mahinang generalization."),
    ("precision_and_recall", "Precision measures correctness among predicted positives; recall measures coverage of actual positives.",
     "Suppose a detector flags 10 items and 8 are truly positive. Precision is 8/10 = 80%. If there are 16 actual positives in total, recall is 8/16 = 50%. Predictions are fairly reliable, but half the positives were missed.\n\nA common mistake is using the same denominator for both metrics. Precision divides by predicted positives; recall divides by actual positives. The appropriate tradeoff depends on the cost of false alarms versus missed detections. Report counts alongside percentages, especially for small samples, so readers can see how much evidence supports the result.",
     "Precision: tama sa predicted positives; recall: nahanap sa actual positives.",
     "Kung 10 items ang na-flag at 8 ang tunay na positive, precision = 8/10 = 80%. Kung 16 ang actual positives sa dataset, recall = 8/16 = 50%. Reliable ang marami sa predictions pero kalahati ng positives ang namiss.\n\nCommon mistake ang pareho ang denominator. Predicted positives sa precision, actual positives sa recall. Depende sa halaga ng false alarms at misses ang tradeoff. Isama ang counts para malinaw kung gaano karami ang evidence sa likod ng percentages."),
    ("lora", "LoRA trains small added matrices while keeping the underlying model weights frozen.",
     "LoRA learns compact updates in selected layers using low-rank matrices. Only these added parameters are optimized; the base weights remain frozen. This reduces trainable parameter count compared with full fine-tuning, although the base model and activations still need memory.\n\nA common mistake is treating an adapter as a complete standalone model or combining it with unrelated base weights. Keep the exact source model and adapter configuration together. For Hachi, measure conversation improvements and existing tool accuracy before promoting a new adapter: fewer trainable parameters do not guarantee that every old behavior will be preserved.",
     "Sa LoRA, maliit na dagdag na matrices ang tine-train habang frozen ang underlying model weights.",
     "Nag-aaral ang LoRA ng compact updates sa selected layers gamit ang low-rank matrices. Added parameters lang ang ino-optimize, pero kailangan pa rin ng memory para sa base model at activations.\n\nCommon mistake ang pag-aakalang kumpletong model na ang adapter o puwede ito sa unrelated base weights. Panatilihin ang exact source model at adapter config. Para sa Hachi, sukatin ang conversation quality at existing tool accuracy bago gamitin ang bagong adapter; hindi garantisado ang preservation ng lahat ng old behavior."),
    ("checkpoints", "A checkpoint saves the state needed to continue training from a completed step.",
     "A resumable checkpoint includes model or adapter weights, optimizer state, scheduler state, progress, and random-number state. Weights alone can support inference but do not restore the exact training process. After saving step 100, reload that checkpoint to continue toward the original total step count.\n\nA common mistake is changing the dataset or schedule while calling it an exact resume. Another is assuming an autosaved file inside a temporary cloud runtime is already a durable backup. Download the recovery archive or confirm it exists in saved notebook output before ending the session.",
     "Ang checkpoint ay save ng state para maipagpatuloy ang training mula sa completed step.",
     "Kasama dapat ang weights, optimizer, scheduler, progress, at random-number state. Weights lang ay useful sa inference pero kulang sa exact resume. Kung saved ang step 100, puwedeng magpatuloy mula roon hanggang sa original total steps.\n\nCommon mistake ang pagbabago ng dataset o schedule habang tinatawag na exact resume. Hindi rin durable backup agad ang autosave sa temporary cloud runtime. I-download ang recovery ZIP o tiyaking nasa saved notebook output bago tapusin ang session."),
    ("fractions", "A fraction expresses a numerator divided by a nonzero denominator.",
     "In 3/4, the numerator is 3 and denominator is 4: three of four equal parts. To add 1/2 and 1/4, rewrite 1/2 as 2/4, then add 2/4 + 1/4 = 3/4.\n\nA common mistake is adding denominators directly, which would incorrectly give 2/6. Addition needs equal-sized parts, so use a common denominator. Multiplication follows a different rule: multiply the numerators and denominators. Check the addition with decimals: 0.5 + 0.25 = 0.75, equal to 3/4. Always identify the operation before choosing a rule.",
     "Ang fraction ay numerator na hinati sa denominator na hindi zero.",
     "Sa 3/4, tatlo sa apat na equal parts ang ibig sabihin. Para idagdag ang 1/2 at 1/4, gawing 2/4 ang 1/2. Kaya 2/4 + 1/4 = 3/4.\n\nCommon mistake ang pag-add ng denominators na maling magiging 2/6. Sa addition, kailangan ng common denominator. Iba ang rule sa multiplication: imultiply ang numerators at denominators. Check gamit ang decimals: 0.5 + 0.25 = 0.75, katumbas ng 3/4. Alamin muna ang operation bago pumili ng rule."),
    ("percentages", "A percentage expresses a value per hundred; 25% means 25 out of 100.",
     "To find 15% of 200, convert 15% to 0.15 and multiply: 0.15 × 200 = 30. For a 15% discount on a 200-peso item, subtract 30, leaving 170 pesos.\n\nA common mistake is subtracting 15 pesos instead of 15%. Also distinguish percentage changes from percentage points: moving from 20% to 25% is 5 percentage points, but a 25% increase relative to the original 20%. State the reference amount so the calculation is unambiguous. Reverse-check a discount by adding the discounted amount to the final price.",
     "Ang percentage ay bilang kada isang daan; 25% ay 25 sa bawat 100.",
     "Para sa 15% ng 200, gawing 0.15 ang 15%: 0.15 × 200 = 30. Kung discount ito, 200 − 30 = 170 pesos ang final price.\n\nCommon mistake ang pag-minus ng 15 pesos sa halip na 15%. Magkaiba rin ang percentage points at percentage change: mula 20% tungong 25% ay dagdag na 5 points, pero 25% increase relative sa 20%. Linawin ang base amount. I-check sa pag-add ng discount pabalik sa final price."),
    ("mean_and_median", "The mean is the sum divided by the count; the median is the middle of sorted values.",
     "For 2, 3, and 10, the mean is (2 + 3 + 10)/3 = 5. The median is 3, the middle sorted value. The large value 10 pulls the mean upward.\n\nFor an even count, average the two middle values: the median of 1, 3, 5, 9 is (3 + 5)/2 = 4. A common mistake is selecting the middle before sorting. Neither statistic is always better. Compare the distribution and purpose: a median can describe a typical value more usefully when a few extreme values strongly affect the mean.",
     "Mean ang sum na hinati sa count; median ang gitnang value pagkatapos i-sort.",
     "Sa 2, 3, at 10, mean = (2 + 3 + 10)/3 = 5. Median ang 3, dahil iyon ang gitna. Nahihila pataas ng malaking 10 ang mean.\n\nKung even ang count, average ng dalawang middle values ang median. Sa 1, 3, 5, 9, median = 4. Common mistake ang hindi pag-sort muna. Walang laging mas mahusay sa dalawa. Tingnan ang distribution at layunin; kung may extreme values, maaaring mas useful ang median bilang typical value."),
    ("active_recall", "Active recall means trying to retrieve an answer from memory before checking your notes.",
     "After reading a section, close the material and answer a question about it. For example, explain why binary search needs sorted data without looking. Then check your explanation and correct missing or mistaken points. This exposes gaps rather than relying only on familiarity with the page.\n\nA common mistake is turning over a flashcard before attempting an answer. Another is repeating only easy questions. Mix difficulty levels and revisit mistakes after a delay. Accurate feedback matters: repeatedly recalling a wrong answer without checking can reinforce that error instead of improving your understanding.",
     "Active recall ang pagsubok sumagot mula sa memory bago tingnan ang notes.",
     "Pagkatapos magbasa, isara ang notes at sumagot sa tanong. Halimbawa, ipaliwanag kung bakit sorted dapat ang data sa binary search. Saka i-check at itama ang kulang o mali. Mas makikita mo ang gaps kaysa sa familiarity lang sa binabasa.\n\nCommon mistake ang pag-flip agad ng flashcard nang hindi sumusubok. Huwag puro madaling tanong; balikan ang mistakes pagkatapos ng delay. Kailangan ang accurate feedback dahil puwedeng makasanayan ang maling sagot kapag hindi ito chine-check."),
]
